bibliography: bibliography.bib
abstract: 'Astronomical texts from Ptolemy to Euler argue geometrically about models: a figure, a few sentences,
  and

  inferences marked by little more than "therefore". This paper gathers what is needed to reconstruct such

  arguments and to check them. It distinguishes what an argument is about (a model, as pure geometry; values for

  its parameters; a state) from what it says (propositions) and what it does (operations: to suppose, to

  construct, to derive, to distinguish cases, to compute, to conclude). It says what licenses a step: an instance

  of a named proposition, an equality by a Common Notion, a fact read off a construction, a calculation, an

  expansion in a small ratio. It treats what a figure supplies without saying so — that points are distinct, lie

  on one side of a line, form a triangle — and what follows when the figure degenerates: cases. It treats

  hypotheses, how to find out whether an argument needs them, and how to detect one used tacitly; equivalence

  and identity of models; exact and approximate steps; quantities computed from a model and parameters determined

  from observations. It fixes a vocabulary for soundness, gives a format in which a reconstructed argument can be

  read at three depths, a procedure in six steps, and a table of rules with Euclid''s wording and its place. Every

  notion is shown on one of three cases: Ptolemy''s solar model in Swerdlow''s exposition, Kepler on the equant,

  and Euler on the tides.

  '
